# PHASE 2 on the M3 — prompt for the agent working there

Read `prompts/base` first, in full. It is the specification. This file adds the
machine specific context and the exact scope for this session.


## Where the work stands

Phases 0 and 1 are implemented and were verified on the M4 Air (32 GB) on
2026-08-15. Read `doc/phase1` for the design decisions and the measurements,
and `AGENTS.md` for the commands. Read the CURRENT STATE section of
`prompts/base` for what was learned and what must not be undone.

The short version: audio capture is a Swift helper writing mono 16 kHz float32
PCM on stdout, one process per source; microphone and system audio stay two
separate streams; recognition is cut on silence; hotkeys live in Hammerspoon and
send commands through `bin/rvwctl` to a unix socket, where a dispatcher runs
them. Nothing about the user experience should change because it is running on
a different Mac.


## This machine

MacBook Pro M3, 96 GB. Two things differ from the Air:

1. The environment is not ready yet. `util/init_local_models.sh` is still
   downloading the model stack, ETA 2026-08-15 23:40 PT. Do not start it again
   and do not assume any model is present. Check first:
   `lms ls`, `lms ps`, `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1/models`,
   and that `.venv/bin/python -c "import mlx_whisper"` succeeds. Until they are
   ready, work on the parts that do not need a model: the screenshot helper, the
   command wiring, the tests, the Hammerspoon bindings.
2. With 96 GB, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B should run without swapping. On the 32 GB Air the
   same model gave about 5 tokens/s and a 129 second explanation, because the
   machine swapped 16.8 GB. Before doing anything else once the models finish,
   re-run the Phase 1 acceptance test here and measure:
   - time from the EXPLAIN hotkey to the first token, and to the last token;
   - `sysctl vm.swapusage` before and after.
   Record the numbers. They decide whether the Air needs a different model or
   whether the whole approach needs a cheaper prompt.

First thing to run when the downloads finish:

    helper/build.sh
    util/run_tests.sh
    bin/rvw --source system --listen        (in one terminal)
    bin/rvwctl EXPLAIN 60                   (in another, after some speech)

`bin/audio_capture` must be built on this machine; it is not in git. Both the
microphone and the system audio tap will prompt for permission the first time,
and the grant goes to the application that launches the helper, so launch it
from the terminal you intend to use.


## Scope for this session

In priority order. Do not start the next item until the previous one runs.

### 1. CLARIFY (alt-cmd-C)

A second explanation command over a shorter window, roughly 30 to 60 seconds,
whose prompt is aimed at reconstructing what was said when the accent or the
recognition made it hard to parse, rather than at explaining concepts. This is
a new prompt builder in `src/rvw/prompts.py` and a new dispatcher command; the
transcript, LLM and streaming code already exist and must not be duplicated.

### 2. SCREENSHOT, SAVE ONLY (alt-cmd-S) — the high priority item

Capture the screen, save it locally with a timestamp, and do nothing else. No
OCR, no LLM, no vision model, no network, and as little visible UI as possible.

Implement it as a separate Swift helper alongside `helper/audio_capture.swift`,
built by the same `helper/build.sh`, with its own usage description in the
embedded Info.plist. Prefer capturing the frontmost application's window with
ScreenCaptureKit; fall back to the whole display only if the window filter turns
out to be awkward, and say so rather than quietly capturing everything.

Save the file with a timestamp and record which application and window it came
from. The image and its metadata are canonical data in the sense of principle 6
in `prompts/base`, so choose a layout now that Phase 3 can adopt unchanged;
`doc/phase1` and the Phase 3 sketch in `prompts/base` describe the intended
`meetings/YYYY/MM/...` shape. Do not build the rest of Phase 3.

Be careful that nothing about this action appears in shared screen content.

### 3. SCREENSHOT, SAVE AND INTERPRET (ctrl-alt-cmd-S) — lower priority

The identical archival save, then optionally a copy plus recent transcript
context to a local vision-language model. Do not delay item 2 for this, and do
not let interpretation failures affect the archival save. The interpretation
must not appear on a shared screen.


## How to work

The user's global rules apply and are strict. In particular:

* TDD: write failing tests first, make them pass, then refactor. Tests are
  plain unittest, run by `util/run_tests.sh`; there is no pytest in the venv.
* snake_case, spaces not tabs, no unicode symbols, functions under about 25
  lines, extract any four or more lines with a single purpose into a named
  function, no comment that merely narrates the following lines.
* Log messages start with "OK " or "FAIL ".
* Wide reaching modes are globals in `src/rvw/config.py`, not parameters.
* Paths are relative to the script, never absolute, and never with a fallback
  location.
* Fix bugs under 50 lines immediately without asking.
* At the end, update `prompts/base` and `doc/` with what was learned, and save
  the session transcript to
  `$dp/git/doc/prompts/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.mm.$HOSTNAME.$IDE_NAME.$TASK`.

Do not refactor working Phase 1 code for tidiness, do not add abstraction layers,
and do not implement anything from Phases 3 to 8 beyond the small metadata
decision noted above.


## Report at the end

What now works, what is rough, what should be tested manually, and whether
anything learned should change the next phase — plus the M3 latency and swap
measurements, since the model choice for both machines depends on them.
